<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaspar de Carvajal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaspar de Carvajal was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> who accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-orellana">Francisco de Orellana</a>'s voyage down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> in 1541–1542. His chronicle of the expedition describes large, densely settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> along the river, such as that of Machiparo, and the women warriors who gave the Amazon its name. Long doubted, his picture of populous riverbank societies has been supported by modern archaeology.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaspar de Carvajal was born in Trujillo and entered the Dominican order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1533 he set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to work for the conversion of its Native peoples. In 1540 he joined, as chaplain, the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, which was searching east of Quito for La Canela, the supposed Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">Cinnamon</a>. Pizarro sent his second in command, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-orellana">Francisco de Orellana</a>, down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napo-river">Napo River</a> with fifty men to look for provisions. Unable to return against the current, Orellana's party followed the river until it reached the estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> in 1542.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Carvajal survived the voyage and wrote it up as the Relación del nuevo descubrimiento del famoso río Grande, recording its dates and many notes on the peoples of the riverbanks: their numbers and dispositions, their tactics of war, rituals, customs and utensils. Gonzalo Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> used parts of it in his Historia general y natural de las <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">Indias</a>, but the work itself stayed obscure until José Toribio Medina published it in 1894. For more than four centuries scholars dismissed its reports of large cities, roads and dense populations as fabrication; research by Prümers and others, published in Nature in 2022, found them very likely correct.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Carvajal became subprior of the Convent of San Rosario in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and in 1544 was chosen to mediate, apparently without success, between the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> and the auditors of the Real Audiencia. He was then sent as a missionary to Tucumán and named protector of the Indians there. In 1553 he became prior of the convent of Huamanga and provincial of Tucumán, and with the Dominicans he brought he founded reducciones de indios and nine Spanish towns. As provincial of Peru from 1557 he wrote to the king about the abuse of Indians in the mines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1565 his province chose him to represent it at the Spanish court and before the Pope, but he probably never crossed the ocean. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1584. Unknowingly, the expedition he served had carried Old World diseases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, malaria and yellow fever into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>. Werner Herzog's 1972 film Aguirre, the Wrath of God shows him as a cowardly chaplain on Lope de Aguirre's later expedition of 1561.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaspar de Carvajal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gaspar de Carvajal</b>, who accompanied Francisco de Orellana's voyage down the Amazon in 1542, described the territory of a chief called Machiparo and densely populated villages lining the riverbanks.</blockquote><a href="/state/omagua">Omagua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trujillo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-5.88&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Trujillo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4405 750 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gaspar de Carvajal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4855 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4855 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-5.88&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
